---
/**
 * Space - working definition of the foundational relational layer.
 * Framed as the configuration space of an identity (physics analogy).
 * Hierarchy proposal: Space → Particles → Time → Mass.
 * Formal metric still open (Critical Gap #44).
 * Keep inline tags on one line with outer spaces (Astro whitespace).
 * Never break before <em>/<strong>/<a> after a word.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ConceptPageHero from '../../components/concepts/ConceptPageHero.astro';
import ConceptPager from '../../components/concepts/ConceptPager.astro';
import { getConcept } from '../../lib/concepts';

const c = getConcept('space');
---

<Layout title={`${c.title} | Identity Engineering`}>
	<main class="min-h-screen">
		<ConceptPageHero
			scene={c.scene}
			tag={`${c.tag} · ${c.layerLabel}`}
			title={c.title}
			lede={c.blurb}
			scrollHint={c.scrollHint}
		/>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-16">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.03] p-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-white/60">
					<strong class="text-white/80">Status:</strong> Working definition. Space is the proposed foundational layer of the framework, framed by analogy with the physical concept of configuration space.
					Dimensionality and metric remain open work
					(<a
						href="https://github.com/identity-engineering/framework/issues/44"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
						class="text-white/75 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50"
					>Critical Gap #44</a>).
					Physics supplies structural analogies, not settled identity laws.
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Physics first</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-4xl">
					Configuration space
				</h2>
				<p>
					In classical mechanics the <strong class="text-white/80">configuration space</strong> of a system is the space of its possible instantaneous configurations
					(see <a
						href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Configuration_space_(physics)"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
						class="text-white/75 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50"
					>configuration space in physics</a>).
					Its dimension equals the number of degrees of freedom.
					Constraints reduce the accessible region; forces and dynamics determine which configurations remain reachable from a given state.
				</p>
				<p>
					Identity Engineering adopts this structure by analogy.
					The configuration space of an identity is the relational arena of its degrees of freedom: relative positions, bindings, access and jurisdiction profiles.
					In the relational tradition (<a
						href="/foundations/leibniz-relational-space"
						class="text-white/75 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50"
					>Leibniz</a>), space is not a container and not a substance.
					It is the system of relations, above all relative distances, that can hold between entities.
				</p>
				<p>
					With this arena the first freedoms appear: freedom with respect to position and relative distance.
					Constraints and forces later reduce those freedoms and produce bound states.
					The freedom itself is born with the configuration space.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Reachable region</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white md:text-4xl">
					What remains open under dynamics
				</h2>
				<p>
					In physics and control theory the <strong class="text-white/80">reachable set</strong> is the set of configurations that can actually be reached from a given state under the available dynamics and controls.
					Earlier drafts of this framework used the phrase "Possibility Space" for a closely related idea.
				</p>
				<p>
					We now treat that idea as the reachable region of the identity's configuration space under the current Stem, Mass, constraints and Vision Gradient.
					No second geometric object is required.
					The arena is the configuration space; the open options are the reachable region inside it.
				</p>
				<p>
					This is why Space sits before Time in the proposed hierarchy.
					At a single moment the Identity Stem is not yet a line. It is an arrangement of bound degrees of freedom.
					Only motion through succession turns those configurations into worldlines, and the bundle of worldlines into the Stem.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<div class="mb-6 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Hierarchy</div>
				<h2 class="mb-8 text-3xl font-bold text-white md:text-4xl">Space → Particles → Time → Mass</h2>
				<ol class="space-y-6 text-lg text-white/55 list-decimal list-inside">
					<li>
						<strong class="text-white/85">Space:</strong> configuration space; birth of degrees of freedom.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong class="text-white/85">Particles:</strong> bound configurations under Strong Binding and Charge (see <a href="/blog/particles-of-identity" class="text-white/75 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50">Particles of Identity</a>).
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong class="text-white/85">Time:</strong> motion of bound configurations; each traces a worldline; the bundle is the Identity Stem.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong class="text-white/85">Mass:</strong> densification of stake around the already moving Stem.
					</li>
				</ol>
				<p class="mt-8 text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
					This order is a pedagogical and engineering order of primitives.
					It is not a denial of spacetime physics. See also the open anchor work on Einstein / spacetime
					(<a
						href="https://github.com/identity-engineering/framework/issues/49"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
						class="text-white/75 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50"
					>Gap #49</a>).
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<div class="mb-6 text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Engineering</div>
				<h2 class="mb-8 text-3xl font-bold text-white md:text-4xl">What we work on</h2>
				<ul class="space-y-6 text-lg text-white/55">
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Degrees of freedom:</strong> which relative positions and distances are still free, and which are already constrained.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Relational arena:</strong> identity aspects exist in relation, not in an empty absolute container.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Binding readiness:</strong> configuration space is the condition for Particles. Without relative position, Strong Binding and Charge have nothing to act on.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Ownership link:</strong> Ownership as relative Access and Jurisdiction is a profile of degrees of freedom with respect to a configuration. Configuration space is the geometric base of that profile.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Reachable region:</strong> which part of the configuration space remains open under current Stem, Mass, constraints and Vision Gradient.
					</li>
				</ul>

				<div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-6">
					<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-white/40 mb-3">Quick self-check</div>
					<ul class="space-y-3 text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
						<li>Which dimensions of my current arrangement still feel free to move?</li>
						<li>Which relative distances are fixed by habit, jurisdiction, or fear?</li>
						<li>Where is the configuration too rigid, or too diffuse to hold a Stem?</li>
						<li>Which region of my configuration space is actually reachable under the forces I currently accept?</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="border-t border-white/10 px-6 py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/55">
				<p>
					Configuration space alone does not define identity. It is the arena.
					Particles bind inside it. Time draws the Stem. Mass densifies stake around that motion.
					Curvature then warps the effective geometry of the reachable region: what looks near or far, costly or cheap.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-6">
					Open formal work:
					<a
						href="https://github.com/identity-engineering/framework/issues/44"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
						class="text-white/75 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50"
					>metric and dimensionality of configuration space (#44)</a>,
					<a
						href="https://github.com/identity-engineering/framework/issues/45"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
						class="text-white/75 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50"
					>residual Possibility Space language (#45)</a>,
					<a
						href="https://github.com/identity-engineering/framework/issues/47"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
						class="text-white/75 underline decoration-white/25 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-white hover:decoration-white/50"
					>Space probes (#47)</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<ConceptPager id="space" />
	</main>
</Layout>
